  "worked_solution": "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of sentences checked until a new typo is found. Since the chance of finding a typo in any sentence is $15%,$ the number of sentences since the previous typo can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=15%=\\frac{15}{100}=0.15.\n$$\n\nSo, $X\u223c\\text{Geom}(0.15).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.15)^{x-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{x-1}(0.15)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding the next typo within the first $3$ sentences is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.85)^{1-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{2-1}(0.15)+(0.85)^{3-1}(0.15) \\\\  & =(0.15)+(0.85)(0.15)+(0.85)^{2}(0.15) \\\\  & =[1+0.85+(0.85)^{2}](0.15) \\\\  & \u22480.386\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.",
